Minor Superficial Abrasion
Common Infections and Afflictions
Denuded epidermis, Serous oozing, Friction mechanism of injury

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-033
Reason for Visit: Large, painful scrape on the knee with embedded gravel.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, highly painful, and contaminated friction injury (road rash) following a fall. The patient is experiencing severe stinging pain and is unable to adequately clean the wound due to distress. Urgent home assessment is mandated to provide local anesthesia, perform thorough, high-pressure mechanical debridement of embedded foreign bodies, and dress the wound appropriately. Delaying this critical decontamination poses a near-certain risk of traumatic tattooing (permanent skin staining) or severe secondary bacterial infection requiring future hospital admission, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I tripped on the driveway and took the skin clean off my knee; the stinging is agonizing, and I can't wash the gravel out.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 1 hour ago following a high-friction shearing injury (fall onto concrete). The patient reports intense, localized stinging and burning pain across the right anterior knee. The wound exhibits a denuded superficial epidermal layer with active serous oozing and minor pinpoint bleeding. The patient notes the wound bed is extremely erythematous and visibly contaminated with embedded dirt and small gravel. Mild surrounding tissue edema is present. Tetanus status is unknown but likely >10 years. Denies deep puncture wounds, severe joint pain restricting movement, or distal numbness.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a limp due to the localized pain of clothing rubbing against the raw wound.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to the pain associated with wound contamination.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly distressed, holding the leg straight to avoid clothing contact.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A negative wound culture would assist in confirming the diagnosis in an uncomplicated cleanly granulating injury, but is entirely unnecessary for this acute traumatic presentation.
Physical Exam: Right anterior knee demonstrates a 8cm x 6cm denuded superficial abrasion. The wound bed is uniformly erythematous, actively weeping serous fluid, and dotted with pinpoint capillary bleeding. The subcutaneous tissue (fat) is definitively intact, confirming a partial-thickness injury. Significant dirt and micro-debris are visibly embedded in the coagulating fibrinous exudate. Active range of motion of the knee is full, though guarded due to pain. No palpable bony step-off or joint effusion. Neurovascular status distal to the injury is entirely normal.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s bathroom lacks adequate sterile supplies for high-pressure irrigation.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has not taken any analgesia.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Minor Superficial Abrasion - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Full-Thickness Laceration - Ruled out definitively as there is no visible gaping of the dermis exposing underlying subcutaneous fat, fascia, or muscle requiring primary closure (sutures).
Prepatellar Bursitis - Unlikely given the precise superficial nature of the injury and the lack of a massive, fluctuant swelling directly over the kneecap.
Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out as the erythema is strictly confined to the immediate margins of the fresh traumatic wound without rapid proximal spreading, and there are no systemic infectious signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with a highly painful, visibly contaminated friction burn. Urgent on-site clinical evaluation was critical to perform a rapid structural assessment of the knee, rule out a deep laceration or underlying fracture, and immediately execute definitive wound decontamination. By administering topical/local analgesia and performing high-pressure irrigation in the home, the foreign bodies were entirely removed, preventing permanent "road rash" tattooing and secondary infection. This essential, time-critical wound management and subsequent specialized dressing application safely averted an unnecessary and highly distressing emergency department visit, fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1000mg orally stat. Performed high-pressure syringe irrigation with 500ml of sterile 0.9% NaCl and mechanical scrubbing to remove all embedded debris. Administered ADT (Diphtheria, Tetanus, Pertussis) booster intramuscularly. Applied a non-adherent, semi-permeable silicone dressing (e.g., Mepitel) covered by an absorbent secondary pad to maintain a moist wound environment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To dramatically speed up healing and prevent a massive, painful scab from forming, leave the specialized silicone dressing on continuously for 5 days without peeking. A scab is simply dead tissue that blocks new skin cells from growing across the wound; keeping the wound bed continually moist under the dressing allows the new skin to slide effortlessly into place, healing twice as fast with minimal scarring.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Provided 3 extra non-adherent dressings and sterile saline for subsequent changes.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner or Practice Nurse in 5 days for the first planned dressing change and wound review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department if the wound begins to rapidly spread a deep red, hot rash up or down the leg, if it starts oozing thick yellow pus, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Deep embedded debris can occasionally be missed, leading to a delayed localized infection.
Clinical Course: The extreme stinging will subside within 24 hours now that the wound is sealed. The injury will produce a large amount of clear/yellowish fluid over the first 48 hours as it heals; this is normal and not an infection. Complete re-epithelialization is expected within 7-14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you notice a red streak travelling from the wound towards your groin, as this indicates a severe infection tracking through your lymphatic system.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The friction literally sanded off the top protective layer of your skin, exposing the nerve endings. The intense cleaning removed the dirt that causes infection and scarring. The dressing acts as artificial skin, protecting those raw nerves and keeping the area moist to promote rapid, scar-free healing.
Actionable Steps: Do not allow the wound to dry out in the open air, and never apply dry gauze directly to the raw tissue, as it will stick like glue and rip the new healing skin off when you remove it.

